WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.760
A macrophage swallows the equivalent of its whole surface about every half hour, yet

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.400
it never shrinks. Where do all that membrane and its receptors go?

3
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.980
Pulling plasma membrane inward as vesicles is called endocytosis.

4
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.960
Each minute, a macrophage pulls in about 3% % of its surface.

5
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.020
Total area divided by uptake rate gives turnover time.

6
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:37.920
Membrane must return just as fast.

7
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:43.263
Receptors ride inside with that membrane, starting at a coated pit.

8
00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:48.923
A curved lattice called clathrin shapes the pit, yet clathrin touches neither

9
00:00:48.923 --> 00:00:50.483
membrane nor receptor.

10
00:00:52.123 --> 00:00:56.883
Adaptor proteins bridge them, gripping a membrane lipid, a receptor tail sequence,

11
00:00:57.103 --> 00:00:58.103
and clathrin.

12
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:01.637
The pit deepens until it hangs by a thin neck.

13
00:01:02.817 --> 00:01:08.177
A large GTP-splitting enzyme called dynamin wraps a collar around that neck.

14
00:01:09.537 --> 00:01:13.797
Hydrolyzing GTP tightens the collar, cutting the neck to free a vesicle.

15
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:19.947
The clathrin coat strips off, and the bare vesicle fuses with the early endosome.

16
00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:25.067
A small switch protein called Rab5 marks this sorting station.

17
00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:31.467
Because the membrane folded inward, the receptor's cytoplasmic tail still points

18
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:32.467
into the cytosol.

19
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.780
Two physical features sort cargo here.

20
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.200
First, proton pumps acidify the inside to about pH 6 to 6.5.

21
00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.280
Many ligands, including LDL, let go in that mild acid.

22
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.960
Second, thin tubes sprout from the round body.

23
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.640
Geometry separates membrane from fluid.

24
00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.600
Surface ÷ volume is 3/R for a sphere, but 2/r for a tube.

25
00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:08.340
Give the tube one-tenth the sphere's radius: 2 ÷ 0.1 = 20, against 3.

26
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.560
That is nearly 7× times more membrane per unit of fluid, so freed ligands stay

27
00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:15.560
mostly in the body.

28
00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:18.063
Now make a prediction.

29
00:02:19.383 --> 00:02:24.743
This receptor carries no tag, no mark telling the cell to destroy it, and its ligand

30
00:02:24.743 --> 00:02:29.963
has let go. Does it stay in the round body, or ride a thin tube back out?

31
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:37.853
It rides a thin tube back out.

32
00:02:39.033 --> 00:02:44.053
No tag is needed, because the tubes hold most of the membrane, so a wandering

33
00:02:44.053 --> 00:02:46.273
receptor usually drifts into one.

34
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.913
Fast tubes marked by Rab4 return receptors in minutes.

35
00:02:52.133 --> 00:02:56.153
A slower route using Rab4 and Rab11 takes tens of minutes.

36
00:02:57.183 --> 00:03:01.823
An LDL receptor repeats that round trip roughly every 10 minutes.

37
00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:05.063
Some cargo heads to the Golgi instead.

38
00:03:06.883 --> 00:03:12.063
Otherwise, early endosomes sort internalized receptors, returning untagged ones to

39
00:03:12.063 --> 00:03:12.483
the surface.
